<p>Relational data modelling is deciding which facts a database records, what one row means, and how rows refer to one another. A shop can put a customer’s name on every order line, or store it once in <code>customers</code> and refer to that row. Both designs can display the same receipt. They differ when the name changes, when a customer has no orders, and when an order is removed.</p>

<p>A useful model makes those changes straightforward. Each fact has a place where it can be recorded without inventing another fact, and a correction has a clear set of rows to change. Keys preserve the distinctions the shop cares about. Relationships say which things belong together. Normalisation checks whether some of those facts have been copied into places where they can disagree.</p>

<p>The examples follow the small tea shop from the <a href="https://staycurrent.dev/learn/databases/relational/">relational introduction</a>. Mina Shah has orders 101 and 103, Tariq Aziz has order 102, and Edda Berg has no orders. Order 103 contains one blue mug, P7, at 20.00 and two packets of green tea, P3, at 4.50 each. Its total is 29.00 and it has no payment. Later sections add small hypothetical catalogue and supplier examples; they do not change those orders.</p>

<h2 id="what-one-row-represents">What one row represents</h2>

<p>Before choosing columns, finish the sentence “One row records …”. One row in <code>orders</code> records one order placed by one customer. One row in <code>order_items</code> records one numbered line on one order. One row in <code>payments</code> records one payment towards one order. These are three different levels of detail, often called the <em>grain</em> of a table.</p>

<p>The difference matters when two things can happen independently. Order 101 has two lines, green tea and a teapot, and two payments, 30.00 by card and 9.50 by gift card. Neither payment is assigned to a particular line. Put a line and a payment in the same row, matching only their order, and each line meets both payments.</p>

