<p>The four rows are a valid answer to “Which line–payment combinations share an order?” They are a poor home for either fact. Adding a payment would require copying every line; adding a line would require copying every payment. An order awaiting payment would need an empty payment field just to hold its lines.</p>

<p>Keep the lines and payments separately, each referring to the order. A report can combine them when needed, taking care to total each set at its own grain first. <a href="https://staycurrent.dev/learn/databases/relational/query-results/">Query results</a> develops that reporting problem. If the shop later allocates a payment to individual lines, that allocation is a new fact, with its own line, payment and amount. It cannot be recovered from the four combinations above.</p>

<h2 id="keys-and-identity">Keys and identity</h2>

<p>A key says which values distinguish one row from every other row the table may contain. The important word is <em>may</em>. Mina, Tariq and Edda happen to have different names; the shop must still be able to register another Mina Shah. Names cannot identify its customers.</p>

<p>A customer’s ID identifies the customer while their name changes. The name describes that customer now. Confusing those roles either makes a correction change identity or prevents two people sharing a name.</p>

<h3 id="candidate-keys-and-surrogate-keys">Candidate keys and surrogate keys</h3>

<p>A <em>superkey</em> is any set of columns that uniquely identifies a row. A <em>candidate key</em> is a minimal superkey: remove any of its columns and the guarantee disappears. “Minimal” concerns unnecessary columns, not how many bytes the key occupies. The <em>primary key</em> is the candidate key chosen as the table’s main identifier; other candidate keys still express real uniqueness rules.</p>

<p>A <em>natural key</em> uses a value that already identifies something in the domain, such as a shop’s established product code. A <em>surrogate key</em> is introduced to identify the row, such as an otherwise meaningless customer number. A surrogate is useful when existing identifiers are missing, wide or liable to change. It does not decide when two rows describe the same thing.</p>

<p>Suppose an order line gains a new <code>line_id</code>. Two rows could then have different line IDs but both claim to be line 2 of order 103. If the shop permits only one such line, it still needs uniqueness on <code>(order_id, line_no)</code>. Adding an ID gives another way to address the line; it does not replace that rule. <a href="https://staycurrent.dev/learn/databases/relational/constraints/">Constraints</a> covers how to declare and maintain these guarantees.</p>

<h3 id="composite-keys">Composite keys</h3>

<p>An order line’s number is local to its order. Orders 101 and 103 both have a line 1, so <code>line_no</code> alone is not a key. Their pair, <code>(order_id, line_no)</code>, is a <em>composite key</em>: a key containing more than one column. The order identifies the list, and the line number identifies an occurrence within it.</p>

<p>Using <code>(order_id, product_id)</code> would impose a different rule: one row per product on an order. Quantity could combine several identical units into that row, but a second occurrence of the same product could not have its own line. That might suit another shop. It is not the rule expressed by numbered order lines.</p>

<p>Try a hypothetical third line on order 103 for another P7, agreed at a different price. The composite key would distinguish <code>(103, 3)</code> from <code>(103, 1)</code>. A key of <code>(103, P7)</code> would collapse that distinction. The core order has only two lines; the proposed third line tests the design’s meaning. No generated line ID can settle whether that occurrence ought to be permitted.</p>

<h2 id="relationships-and-cardinality">Relationships and cardinality</h2>

<p>A relationship says which rows belong together. Its <em>cardinality</em> describes how many can participate. Read it in both directions, separating the minimum from the maximum: an order belongs to exactly one customer, while a customer can have no orders or many.</p>

<p>Figure 2 reads the shop’s references in words. Arrows point from a row carrying a reference to the row it identifies. “Exactly one” and “zero or many” describe permitted participation; Edda and Mina show two actual cases.</p>

